sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If a solid angle is contained by three plane angles, then the sum of any two is greater than the remaining one.
----
proof:

Let the solid angle at {A} be contained by the three plane angles {BAC|angBAC}, {CAD|angCAD}, and {DAB|angDAB}.

I say that the sum of any two of the angles {BAC|angBAC}, {CAD|angCAD}, and {DAB|angDAB} is greater than the remaining one.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="270" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.20 diagram" src="propXI20.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: the solid angle at A seen from above, its three faces
    // drawn as the filled triangles ABC, ABD, ACD, and AE drawn in the face
    // ABC making the angle BAE equal to DAB.
    //
    // The walk needs AB, AC, AD before BC, DB, DC are joined, so those six
    // are solid black slide twins over Joyce's triangle edges, hidden at
